    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M8 V03: Stellar spectra and classes. How can spectra classify stars and reveal their temperature? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "First, the three kinds of spectra, now explained with photons. A hot, dense object gives a continuous spectrum, every wavelength. A hot, thin gas gives an emission spectrum, bright lines only. Its electrons can only have fixed energy levels. Each drop between levels gives out one photon, with E equals h f, an exact energy. Hydrogen's red line at six hundred and fifty-six nanometres comes from a drop of three point zero times ten to the minus nineteen joules, about one point nine electron volts. Now put cooler, thinner gas in front of a hot source. Its atoms absorb only photons whose energy exactly matches a gap between their levels, lifting an electron up. They give that energy out again, but in all directions, so less reaches you at that wavelength. That's a dark line, at the same wavelength as the bright one. A star works like this. Its hot, dense layers give the continuous light, and the cooler gas above absorbs, so stars show absorption spectra. How does a star's spectrum compare with a pure black body? Plot intensity against wavelength. A black body gives a smooth curve, and its shape depends only on temperature. A star's spectrum follows nearly the same curve, but with narrow dips cut into it. The curve tells you the temperature. The dips tell you which atoms are in the atmosphere, and, as you'll see, they tell you the temperature again.",
    "conversationalApproach": "Here's the Sun's light, spread into a spectrum. A smooth rainbow, with dark lines cut into it. From this one strip, astronomers can read a star's temperature, and sort it into a class. Almost every star is mostly hydrogen. So which stars show the strongest hydrogen lines? The hottest, the coolest, or ones in between? Make your prediction. Ones in between, and the reason is the key to classifying stars. So how can spectra classify stars and reveal their temperature? First, the three kinds of spectra, now explained with photons. A hot, dense object gives a continuous spectrum, every wavelength. A hot, thin gas gives an emission spectrum, bright lines only. Its electrons can only have fixed energy levels. Each drop between levels gives out one photon, with E equals h f, an exact energy. Hydrogen's red line at six hundred and fifty-six nanometres comes from a drop of three point zero times ten to the minus nineteen joules, about one point nine electron volts. Now put cooler, thinner gas in front of a hot source. Its atoms absorb only photons whose energy exactly matches a gap between their levels, lifting an electron up. They give that energy out again, but in all directions, so less reaches you at that wavelength. That's a dark line, at the same wavelength as the bright one. A star works like this. Its hot, dense layers give the continuous light, and the cooler gas above absorbs, so stars show absorption spectra.",
    "openingDecision": "opens on v02's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"); first beat: the four evidence tags and v02's red hand tag \"same tool, new target\" fade; the painted Sun (about 360 px), the strip, the curve and the \"T = ?\" gauge stay, all solid. On \"A smooth rainbow, with dark lines cut into it\" the strip's dark lines pulse once, ink pen ring round the Hα line, ink hand tag \"dark lines: missing light\". On \"Almost every star is mostly hydrogen\" chip \"stars: mostly hydrogen (about 3/4 by mass)\". On \"prediction\" a 3 s `Countdown` beside chips \"hottest stars\", \"coolest stars\", \"in between\"; three star plates (`y12p-star-hot-blue-v1.png`, `y12p-star-white-v1.png`, `y12p-star-cool-red-v1.png`, about 220 px each) appear above the chips. On \"Ones in between\" the \"in between\" chip ticks under the white star, red hand tag \"surprise: not the hottest\". On \"temperature\" the chip becomes the heading: \"How can spectra classify stars and reveal their temperature?\"; the \"T = ?\" gauge stays.",
    "understandingCheck": "Here's the Sun's light, spread into a spectrum. A smooth rainbow, with dark lines cut into it. From this one strip, astronomers can read a star's temperature, and sort it into a class. Almost every star is mostly hydrogen. So which stars show the strongest hydrogen lines? The hottest, the coolest, or ones in between? Make your prediction. Ones in between, and the reason is the key to classifying stars. So how can spectra classify stars and reveal their temperature? Your turn. A star's spectrum peaks at seven hundred and twenty-five nanometres. It shows strong lines from metals, and weak molecular bands. Calculate its surface temperature, and identify its spectral class. Pause here to answer. Last one. Star P shows strong lines of ionised helium. Star Q shows strong titanium oxide bands. Both have weak hydrogen lines, and both have the same composition. Explain why both stars have weak hydrogen lines, and identify their spectral classes. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M8 V03: Stellar spectra and classes. How can spectra classify stars and reveal their temperature? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 192 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,303 characters, 17 scenes, about 543 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v02's final frame.\n\nScope: third video of Year 12 Physics Module 8 (From the Universe to the Atom, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), inquiry question \"What evidence is there for the origins of the elements?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m8-v01.md`; `math-a12-d10.md` for the calculation layout. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here (2017 wording from the ledger, site values kept, every number recalculated in Python, line wavelengths from NIST, original attempts, asset reuse). Site lesson read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extract `work/brief168/module8-lesson09.sa.txt`, hash checked against the live file): orient and predict (dark lines prompt, warm-up, intentions, vocabulary including Fraunhofer lines), read the three spectra (Kirchhoff's laws, Figure 1), classify with OBAFGKM (table, Figure 2, mnemonic, subclasses, luminosity classes), measure motion from a wavelength shift (radial velocity, exoplanets), identify stars with care (HSC tip, Activity 1), calculate radial velocities (Activity 2, misconceptions, \"Account 7 marks\", \"Compare 6 marks\"), independent practice, retrieve and reflect (Payne, 1925). `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module8/lesson09.sa.html`, SHA256 `8c9204993c58f79ba383b7a39356ab3176285682c4d2cdc7a92a639847ca3796` (recomputed 6 Nov; matches the route). Route entry: `phys-y12-m8-v03`, sections `read-the-three-spectra`, `classify-with-obafgkm`, `identify-stars-with-care`. Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Revisit continuous, emission and absorption mechanisms; classify stellar spectra with ionisation/temperature reasoning. Stop before HR evolution tracks.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m8-v04: How does the HR diagram distinguish brightness, temperature and stellar stage?\"\n\nOwner rules applied: 2017 syllabus quoted exactly; no people, hands, faces or body outlines (Cecilia Payne and earlier classifiers are named in words only, never drawn; no astronomer at an eyepiece); spectra, intensity curves, energy levels, the class strip and calculation strips are coded graphs, never painted; the stars, filament lamp and discharge tube are painted plates (all delivered and inspected 7 Nov), scaled uniformly, never stretched; line wavelengths from NIST (Hα 656.3, Hβ 486.1, Hγ 434.0, Hδ 410.2 nm; Ca II K 393.4 and H 396.8 nm; He I 447.1 and 587.6 nm; He II 468.6 nm; Na D 589.0 and 589.6 nm (the absorption example in scene 3); TiO band heads 476, 517, 545, 616, 671, 706 nm); every number recalculated in Python with h = 6.626 × 10⁻³⁴ J s, c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹, b = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m K, 1 eV = 1.602 × 10⁻¹⁹ J; data-sheet symbols E = hf, λ_max = b/T; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback with one crossed-out mistake; original attempts; Australian spelling; no em dashes. Recalled, not re-taught: m7-v03 (spectroscope; three spectra in plain language; re-emission in all directions; fingerprints; Fraunhofer lines), m7-v04 (temperature from continuum colour and line strengths in outline; the O to M sequence named; Doppler shifts), m7-v09 (black body, Wien's law), m7-v10 (E = hf, eV), v02 (the black-body CMB; spectra as evidence).\n\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 8; ledger ids `P8-IQ1-04`, `P8-IQ1-05`), quoted from the route JSON `syllabusActions` and the ledger:\n\n- `P8-IQ1-04`: \"account for the production of emission and absorption spectra and compare these with a continuous black body spectrum (ACSPH137)\"\n- `P8-IQ1-05`: \"investigate the key features of stellar spectra and describe how these are used to classify stars\"\n\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 62)\n\n**Boundary:** starts on v02's final frame (the Sun's spectrum strip and intensity curve with dips, \"T = ?\"). Teaches: the production of continuous, emission and absorption spectra, now with photons (E = hf; Hα's 1.89 eV gap; cool sodium vapour absorbing 589 nm from its lowest level); why absorption lines are dark (re-emission in all directions, recalled); a star's spectrum as a near black-body curve with dips, compared with a pure black body (the curve gives temperature; the dips give composition and temperature); temperature method 1, Wien's law (Sun: 500 nm, 5800 K) and its limits for hot stars; method 2, which lines are strong: the hydrogen line hump explained by level-2 population and ionisation; ionised helium (hottest), ionised calcium (Sun-like), titanium oxide bands (coolest); the classes O B A F G K M with approximate temperature ranges (M below about 3500 K, root decision 3) and colours, subclasses 0 to 9, the Sun as G2; why the letters are out of order; Payne's result (classes are a temperature sequence, not a composition sequence); a worked classification (414 nm, 7000 K, F). Stops before luminosity classes and the HR diagram (v04; the handoff names the problem), radial velocity calculations (m7-v04 taught them; the site's Doppler card is not repeated) and exoplanets (site extension).\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"How can spectra classify stars and reveal their temperature?\" The answer: a star's spectrum is a near black-body curve, whose peak gives temperature (5); absorption lines cut into it, and which lines are strong depends on temperature because temperature sets which energy levels are occupied and which atoms are ionised (6, 7); sorting by those lines gives the classes O to M, a temperature sequence (8, 9).\n- **Hook (site predict \"Why do these dark lines appear at specific wavelengths\", turned to a surprise).** 3 s prediction chips: \"hottest stars\", \"coolest stars\", \"in between\". Answer: in between (about 10 000 K, A stars). This sets up scene 6's cause.\n- **Production of spectra (`P8-IQ1-04`; site `read-the-three-spectra`, Kirchhoff's three laws).** m7-v03 taught the three spectra before photons; this design adds the photon account: each drop between fixed levels emits one photon of energy E = hf equal to the gap; Hα at 656.3 nm is a 3.03 × 10⁻¹⁹ J (1.89 eV) gap. Kirchhoff is named on screen only (\"Kirchhoff's laws\", as the site). Plates reused from m7-v03 for the sources.\n- **Why dark (site \"Account 7 marks\" step 4).** Absorbed energy is re-emitted in all directions, so less reaches the observer at that wavelength; recalled in one sentence from m7-v03, with the same arrow visual.\n- **Absorber (Revision 2, brief 192 finding 2).** Scene 3's cool gas is sodium vapour, whose 589.0 nm line (2.11 eV) is absorbed by electrons in its lowest level, so a cool gas absorbs it without any excited population. Revision 1 drew cool hydrogen absorbing Hα from n = 2, which needs electrons already in n = 2; scene 6 explains why cool hydrogen has almost none. Hydrogen's Hα returns only in scene 6, where the temperature argument supplies the n = 2 population.\n- **Comparison with a black body (`P8-IQ1-04` \"compare\"; site \"Account 7 marks\" step 5).** Coded intensity graph: a smooth Planck curve, then a stellar curve following it with narrow dips. \"The curve tells you the temperature; the dips tell you which atoms, and the temperature again.\"\n- **Method 1: Wien (m7-v09 recalled).** Sun: peak about 500 nm, T = 2.898 × 10⁻³ / 5.0 × 10⁻⁷ = 5800 K (site 5800 K; author note a). Limits stated: hot stars peak in the ultraviolet (absorbed by our atmosphere) and dust reddens starlight, so lines are also used.\n- **Method 2: line strengths (site `identify-stars-with-care` and \"Compare 6 marks\" step 4).** The visible (Balmer) lines need an electron in level 2 (10.2 eV above the lowest level). Cool: almost all in level 1. Hotter: collisions lift more to level 2. Too hot: ionised (13.6 eV), no electron. Best near 10 000 K (A stars). Narration says \"the balance is best\", not \"most atoms are in level 2\" (author note b). The site trap \"weak hydrogen lines mean little hydrogen\" is narrated and crossed.\n- **Other features (site table \"Spectral Types, Temperature and Dominant Features\").** He II in O stars (helium holds its electrons more tightly: 24.6 eV against 13.6 eV); neutral He in B (on screen); Ca II H and K strong in G and K (calcium ionises at 6.1 eV); TiO bands below about 3500 K (molecules survive only in cool atmospheres; the site's \"M < 3 700 K\" stays in author note c). \"Each feature works like a thermometer reading over its own range.\"\n- **Classes (site `classify-with-obafgkm`; Revision 2, root decision 3).** O B A F G K M with approximate ranges on screen (O > 30 000 K; B 10 000 to 30 000; A 7500 to 10 000; F 6000 to 7500; G 5200 to 6000; K about 3500 to 5200; M below about 3500), each strip tagged \"approximate\"; colours blue to red; subclasses 0 to 9; Sun G2. Narration, screen, feedback, notes and model answers all use \"about 3500 K\" for the K/M boundary; the site's 3700 K is in author note c only. The site's mnemonic is not used (common versions are unsuitable; m7-v04 made the same call); the memory aid is the hydrogen-hump rule instead. Luminosity classes (V, III, I) are left to v04.\n- **Why the letters are out of order (history, accurate form).** The early Harvard scheme lettered spectra by hydrogen-line strength; astronomers later reordered them into a smooth sequence of features, which turned out to be a temperature sequence (author note d). Names: Cecilia Payne (1925) is named in scene 9, as the site does; the classifiers are in author note d.\n- **Payne's result (site retrieve and reflect).** Classes differ through temperature, not composition; almost all stars are mostly hydrogen and helium (site misconception \"Spectral type depends only on chemical composition\").\n- **Worked classification (original).** Peak 414 nm, strong but not maximal hydrogen, some metal lines, no ionised helium, no molecular bands: T = 2.898 × 10⁻³ / 4.14 × 10⁻⁷ = 7000 K, class F; the two methods agree. Two stages, givens grouped.\n- **Doppler and exoplanets (site `measure-motion-with-doppler-shift`; not in the route sections).** Not repeated: m7-v04 derived and used Δλ/λ = v/c, and v02 used it. One screen tag in scene 4 (\"line positions also give speed: m7-v04\").\n- **Memory aid (scene 11, new): \"Hydrogen peaks in the middle: too cool to lift, too hot to keep.\"** See \"Memory aid check\".\n- **Attempts (original, HSC style).** A1 (3 marks): peak at 725 nm, strong metal lines, weak molecular bands: T = 4.0 × 10³ K, class K. Crossed: substituting 725 (nm) for metres gives 4.0 × 10⁻⁶ K. A2 (3 marks, explain): two stars of the same composition, one with ionised helium and one with titanium oxide, both with weak hydrogen lines. Crossed: \"weak hydrogen lines mean little hydrogen\".\n- **Significant figures.** Two figures for temperatures from peaks (peaks read to two or three figures).\n- **Handoff (route next; Revision 2).** Two M stars with the same temperature, so the same continuum peak and the same class, but luminosities a million times apart: temperature alone cannot say how big or how luminous a star is. The design no longer says the two spectra match completely, because m7-v04 used pressure-broadened lines to tell dwarfs from giants; v04 opens on the same wording. \"Next: how does the HR diagram distinguish brightness, temperature and stellar stage?\". See \"Final frame\".\n- **Colour key (spectra as m7-v03):** spectrum strips coloured by wavelength on a 380 to 750 nm axis (extended to 350 nm when the calcium lines are shown), dark lines black; intensity curves amber (black body) and ink (star); energy levels navy, electrons rose, photons amber packets; class swatches blue (O) to red (M); crossed claims red.\n- **Colour key (Revision 2, fixed for every scene).** As above. Spectrum continua and emission lines true to wavelength: 393.4 and 396.8 nm violet (near the edge of vision), 410.2 nm violet, 434.0 nm blue-violet, 447.1 nm blue, 468.6 nm blue, 486.1 nm cyan, 587.6 and 589 nm yellow-orange, 656.3 nm red; anything below 380 nm is drawn as a grey \"UV\" band, never coloured. A photon packet takes its line's true colour when it is a visible line (scene 2's Hα photon red, scene 3's sodium photon yellow-orange); otherwise photons are amber. Temperature T and the slider red; wavelength λ_max teal; class letters ink with their swatches; framed answers in a dark-ink box; crossed chips and their hand tags red. Star swatches: O blue, B blue-white, A white, F yellow-white, G yellow-white, K orange, M orange-red. Hand-font tags take the colour of the quantity they mark.\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Current working line at least 44 px (scenes 2, 5 and 10 stages and feedback rows 52 px; framed answers 56 px); live readouts (the temperature slider's T, λ_max and colour; the \"T = ?\" gauge) about 52 px; class-strip labels, ranges and chips 30 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; question text 36 px; key notes 34 px; nothing a student must read below 26 px. All text above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Anchor (Revision 2).** v02's final frame is the anchor: the painted Sun (`phys-y11-sun-v1.png`, inspected 7 Nov) with its spectrum strip and \"T = ?\". The three painted stars (`y12p-star-hot-blue-v1.png`, `y12p-star-white-v1.png`, `y12p-star-cool-red-v1.png`, 1254 × 1254 RGBA, inspected 7 Nov) join it in scene 1, carry the slider (5), the panels (6), the class strip (8) and the cards, and the cool-red star closes the lesson (16). The painted filament lamp `phys-y11-filament-lamp-v1.png` and discharge tube `y12p-discharge-tube-v1.png` (inspected) carry 2 and 3.\n- **Simulation (Revision 2).** The core live control is the star thermometer in scene 5: a red temperature slider on the intensity graph moves the black-body curve, with live readouts T, λ_max = b/T and a colour swatch, and the painted star beside it switches plate as the slider passes each range (Python: 3000 K → 966 nm, cool-red star; 4000 K → 725 nm; 5800 K → 500 nm, the Sun; 7000 K → 414 nm; 10 000 K → 290 nm, white star; 30 000 K → 96.6 nm, hot-blue star, the peak in the grey UV band). The strip under the graph shows the hydrogen lines growing and fading with the slider (strongest near 10 000 K), previewing scene 6. Scene 6's three atom panels and scene 7's feature thermometers are the other live elements.\n- **Board stages (Revision 2).** Every scene has its own stage: the Sun and strip with three stars (1), lamp and tube with the ladder (2), the absorption bench (3), the intensity graph (4) becoming the live slider (5; 4 and 5 about 63 s), the atom panels and hump (6), the feature thermometers (7), the class strip (8, with 9 about 61 s), working (10), the panels again (11). No set-up runs past about 65 s.\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a picture in one move (A1: an ink intensity curve with a peak marker and an unlabelled spectrum strip with dark metal lines and faint bands; A2: the painted hot-blue star \"P\" and cool-red star \"Q\", each above an unlabelled strip, P with a dark line at 468.6 nm and weak hydrogen lines, Q with broad bands and weak hydrogen lines). On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the data (A1: \"λ_max = 725 nm; b = 2.898 × 10⁻³ m K; strong metal lines, weak molecular bands\"; A2: \"same composition; P: He II strong; Q: TiO strong; both: weak H\"), results are framed and one classic slip is crossed.\n- **Site sources used:** lesson 9: predict (dark lines at specific wavelengths) (1, 3); vocabulary (absorption, emission, spectral type, Fraunhofer lines) (2, 3, 8); `read-the-three-spectra` (Kirchhoff's laws, stellar spectra are absorption spectra, temperature-dependent ions) (2, 3, 6); `classify-with-obafgkm` (sequence, ranges, dominant features, colours, Sun G2, subclasses) (7, 8, 10); `identify-stars-with-care` (O stars show He II; M stars show TiO; presence of lines more reliable than strength) (7, 10); misconceptions (spectral type is temperature, not composition; blueshift does not mean hotter) (9, 4 tag); \"Account 7 marks\" (continuum, cooler layer, quantised levels, re-emission, comparison, Fraunhofer) (2 to 4); \"Compare 6 marks\" (O against M; why hydrogen is not the discriminator) (6, 7, 14, 15); retrieve and reflect (Payne 1925, 300 spectra) (9). Not used: the mnemonic (above), luminosity classes (v04), radial velocity and exoplanet cards (m7-v04; site extension), example stars Mintaka, Procyon and Arcturus (author note c).\n- **Site notes (quoted from site lesson 9, read 6 Nov; for root to log in `../site-corrections-phys-y12.md` if accepted; v01 and v02 list notes 1 to 8, so these continue the numbering):**\n  9. Contradiction on O-star hydrogen lines: the table says \"O (>30,000 K): Ionised He lines\", Activity 1 says \"A star shows no hydrogen lines, but strong ionised helium (He II) lines. State its spectral type\", and the Q2 model answer says the O star is \"hot enough to ionise hydrogen completely, removing Balmer lines\"; but \"Compare 6 marks\" (correctly) says the O star shows \"weak hydrogen lines\". O stars do show weak Balmer lines. Suggest \"weak hydrogen lines\" in the Activity and the model answer.\n  10. Example star (site table): \"O (>30,000 K): ... E.g. Mintaka.\" Mintaka's primary is O9.5 II at about 29 500 K, at the cool edge of class O. Suggest a clearer O example such as Naos (ζ Puppis, O4, about 40 000 K) or keep Mintaka with \"late O\".\n  11. Precision: \"the Sun is 73% hydrogen and 25% helium\" (retrieve and reflect, attributed to Payne 1925). Payne showed hydrogen and helium dominate stellar atmospheres; the mass fractions quoted are modern values. Suggest \"showed that stars are made mostly of hydrogen and helium\".\n  12. Checked and correct: Kirchhoff's three laws; re-emission in all directions; class order and ranges; Sun G2V; Ca II K 393.3 nm (NIST 393.37 nm); 658.1 nm gives z = 0.00274 and 820 km s⁻¹.\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) electrons in atoms have fixed energy levels, so a thin gas emits or absorbs photons of exact energies E = hf; a cool gas absorbs from its lowest level (sodium at 589 nm) (2, 3); (2) a star's hot dense layers give a near black-body continuum, and its cooler atmosphere absorbs, so a star shows absorption lines on a black-body curve (3, 4); (3) the curve's peak gives temperature through Wien's law, but hot stars peak in the ultraviolet (5); (4) temperature sets how many electrons are lifted to each level and which atoms are ionised, so it sets which lines are strong (6, 7); (5) sorting by those lines gives O to M, a temperature sequence (8); (6) composition is nearly the same for all classes (9).\n- **Entry knowledge (route `entryKnowledge`):** v02 (spectra as evidence; black body); m7-v03 (spectra in plain language; fingerprints); m7-v04 (temperature from colour and lines in outline; Doppler); m7-v09 (Wien's law); m7-v10 (E = hf; eV).\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary (plain meaning at first use):** energy level (recalled: one of the fixed energies an electron in an atom can have, 2); photon (recalled, 2); continuous, emission, absorption spectrum (recalled, 2, 3); black body (recalled, 4); ionised (an atom that has lost one or more electrons, 6); spectral class (a star's group, O to M, set by its spectrum, 8); subclass (8); molecular band (a broad dark band from molecules, 7).\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. Explainer family `m8-spectra` (absorption from a cooler layer between a hot source and a dispersed spectrum; src/explainer/physics/module8) supplies the source, gas and strip in scene 3, within its limit \"schematic bands and lines, no element-specific wavelengths\": this design supplies the NIST wavelengths and the classification.\n- **New explainer motion:** **energy ladder with photon** (hydrogen levels 1, 2, 3 and the ionisation line, an electron dropping or jumping, a photon leaving or arriving with its E = hf readout); **intensity graph with dips** (a black-body curve and a stellar curve with narrow dips aligned to the strip); **level-2 population meter** (three side-by-side atom panels, \"cool\", \"about 10 000 K\", \"very hot\", each showing where electrons sit, and a hump graph of Balmer line strength against temperature); **class strip** (seven spectrum strips O to M stacked, with the key features marked and coloured swatches); **feature thermometers** (bars showing the temperature range over which each feature is strong: He II, He I, H, Ca II, TiO); `AlignedAlgebra`; `NumberTicker`; `Countdown` (3 s in scene 1; 10 s in scenes 12 and 14); `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; the spectrum strip and intensity graph centre and right; star or source plates at the left; the feature thermometers at the lower right from scene 7 to scene 10; everything above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\n  (a) The Sun's effective temperature is 5772 K (IAU 2015 nominal); its Wien peak is 502 nm. The site's 5800 K and a 500 nm peak are kept. The solar spectrum's measured peak per unit wavelength is near 500 nm; per unit frequency it is in the near infrared, which is not mentioned.\n  (b) By the Boltzmann factor, the fraction of neutral hydrogen with n = 2 is only about 10⁻⁵ even near 10 000 K; the Saha ionisation balance then sets the maximum of n₂/N_total near 9500 to 10 000 K. Narration: \"collisions lift more electrons up to level two\" and \"the balance is best near ten thousand kelvin\" are true; \"most atoms\" is never said.\n  (c) K/M boundary (Revision 2, root decision 3, brief 192 finding 3). Learner copy says \"about 3500 K\" everywhere (narration, class strip, thermometer bar, feedback, notes, model answers). The site's table uses 3700 K, and many texts put the K/M boundary at about 3700 to 3900 K; boundaries are approximate conventions, so every on-screen range carries \"approximate\". The 3700 K value stays in this note only.\n  (g) Composition bars (Revision 2, brief 192 finding 4). Approximate solar mass fractions: hydrogen about 73%, helium about 25%, everything else about 2% (total 100%). Scene 1's chip \"about 3/4 by mass\" is a rounded single statement, not a stacked whole.\n  (d) The Harvard scheme (Williamina Fleming, Edward Pickering, 1890) used letters A to Q in order of hydrogen-line strength; Antonia Maury and Annie Jump Cannon reordered and merged them (Cannon 1901 to 1912: O B A F G K M with decimal subclasses); Cecilia Payne (1925 thesis) used Meghnad Saha's ionisation theory to show the sequence is one of temperature and that stars are mostly hydrogen and helium. Only Payne is named in narration.\n  (e) He I lines peak in B stars (about 20 000 K); He II requires He⁺ (24.6 eV to remove the first electron), present only above about 30 000 K. Ca II H and K peak in G and K stars; Ca ionisation energy 6.11 eV. TiO dissociation energy about 6.9 eV; bands strengthen below about 4000 K.\n  (f) Pronunciations: \"H alpha\"; \"titanium oxide\"; \"Kirchhoff\" as \"keer-koff\" (screen only); \"Payne\" as \"pain\".\n- **Open decisions for root:**\n  - **1. Mnemonic (root).** No O to M sentence mnemonic (the site's \"Oh Be A Fine Girl/Guy, Kiss Me\" is not used; m7-v04 made the same call). Keep,